# Keep the IR sensor's control node root-only.
#
# logind's uaccess tag hands the seat user read-write access to every camera
# node, which is right for the RGB webcam and wrong for the sensor that
# authenticates. What this rule closes: with write access to the sensor's
# subdev a process running as the user could change exposure or gain under
# the daemon mid-capture and drive the strobe itself. After it, exposure,
# gain and the strobe are root-only. Only faceauthd (root) needs this node.
#
# The capture node the IR frames arrive on (/dev/videoN, "ipu3-cio2 N" for
# the IR port) is not in this file. Face setup locks it with a rule of its
# own (Mike's ruling, fix round 11), /etc/udev/rules.d/72-faceauth-ir-node.rules,
# which `faceauth cam ir-rule` prints from faceauth's own camera detection
# and face removal deletes (then `faceauth cam ir-reset` gives the node back
# root's, the video group's and no ACL, and add events, which alone set a
# video node's group, have uaccess grant the seat user afresh; fix round
# 13): exactly the node faceauth reads its IR frames
# from, only when it carries infrared or grey formats and no colour one,
# pinned by attributes that do not move (a CIO2 port by its name and the
# IPU3's PCI function; a USB camera by its interface and node index on one
# line, which marks it, and its port, vendor and product on the next, since
# udev matches one line's parent keys on one ancestor alone), never by
# number. Anything unclear leaves the node open, and
# `faceauth doctor` (camera.ir_capture) says why. While it is open, starting
# a stream on it makes the kernel start the linked sensor, so a process
# running as the user can plausibly take ambient IR frames with the
# illuminator off, and any process that holds it (a browser sharing the
# camera through PipeWire) makes a face attempt fall to the password until
# it lets go. Neither rule ever touches the RGB webcam, the CSI-2 receiver
# subdevs or /dev/media0: the RGB webcam shares the receiver, and the
# port-to-sensor map is firmware-specific.
#
# The file name is the ordering. 70-uaccess.rules adds the tag and
# 73-seat-late.rules turns the tag into the ACL, so this file must sort between
# them: a 70- name sorts before 70-uaccess and the tag comes straight back.
#
# The sensor is matched by its sysfs name, never by node number: v4l-subdevN
# moves between boots and module reloads. GROUP and MODE are set as well as
# the tag removed, so a user in the video group does not keep write access
# through the group bits, and MODE="0600" sets the ACL mask to nothing, so an
# access entry granted before this rule was installed is inert even before
# the next reboot. doctor reads the node's live ACL and the udev database's
# current tags, not this file.
#
# A UVC IR camera (a USB module rather than an IPU3 sensor) has no v4l-subdev
# node; its control path is its capture node, which the setup rule above
# covers when the camera is the one faceauth reads (the config's ir_video)
# and carries infrared alone, pinned by its USB interface, port, vendor and
# product and the node's index, so two identical modules (front and rear, or
# a dock) are told apart and its RGB sibling, which can also be index 0 on
# another interface, stays open.

ACTION=="remove", GOTO="faceauth_ir_end"
SUBSYSTEM=="video4linux", KERNEL=="v4l-subdev*", ATTR{name}=="ov7251 *", TAG-="uaccess", GROUP="root", MODE="0600"
LABEL="faceauth_ir_end"
